AACSearch comes with a few built-in tools and methods that easily debug and investigate issues on your AACSearch stack environment.

# Doctor CLI {% #doctor-cli %}

The doctor CLI helps you validate your server health and best practices. Using the Doctor CLI, you can verify your server configuration for best practices, validate your AACSearch stack connectivity and storage read and write access, and available storage space.

To run the Doctor check, simply run the following command from your terminal. You might need to replace 'aacsearch' with your AACSearch Docker container ID. To find out what's your container ID, you can run `docker ps` command (more on that, in the next section).

```bash
docker exec aacsearch doctor
```

# Logs {% #logs %}

Checking your AACSearch containers can be a great way to pinpoint where and what exactly happens inside your AACSearch services. You can list your AACSearch containers using the following command in your terminal:

```bash
docker ps
```

The output of this command will show you a list of all your running Docker containers, their ID's, uptime, and open ports. You can use each container ID to get a list of all the container `stdout` and `stderr` logs by using the following command:

```bash
docker logs [CONTAINER-ID]
```

# Status codes {% #status-codes %}

AACSearch uses conventional HTTP response codes to indicate the success or failure of an API request. In general: Codes in the 2xx range indicate success. Codes in the 4xx range indicate an error that failed given the information provided (e.g., a required parameter was omitted, invalid input, etc.). Codes in the 5xx range indicate an error with the AACSearch server, but these are rare.

{% arrow_link href="/docs/advanced/platform/response-codes" %}
Learn more about AACSearch status codes
{% /arrow_link %}

# Development mode {% #development-mode%}

When moving to dev mode, your server will produce much more verbose error messages. Instead of getting a general 500 error, you'll be able to view the exact error that happened on the server, debug the issue further or [report it to the AACSearch team](https://github.com/aacsearch/aacsearch/issues/new?body=500%20Error).

To change your dev environment, edit your server `_APP_ENV` environment variable from 'production' to 'development' in your `.env` file located in the `aacsearch` directory in the location where you first installed AACSearch.

```text
_APP_ENV=development
_APP_OPENSSL_KEY_V1=your-secret-key
_APP_DOMAIN=localhost
```
